WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:33.440
I

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:59.940
know very well that we are not covering them
in chronological order, but one at the time

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.210
we are covering the most interesting one.

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:05.630
I am talking of course about the Roman Emperors.

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:10.109
Roman Emperors were men who wielded immense
power in a wide variety of ways.

00:01:10.109 --> 00:01:11.340
Some were good military leaders.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:14.500
Some ruled fairly and openly, to the benefit
of their citizens.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:19.800
Some were a destructive force at the centre
of plots, conspiracies, and murder.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.500
Today’s protagonist was all of the above.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:27.270
In many ways a puzzling man, he was not born
to become an Emperor, and when he did become

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:32.320
one, he appeared reluctant, and spent almost
half of his reign in self-imposed exile.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:37.479
This is the story of Tiberius, the second
Roman Emperor.

00:01:37.479 --> 00:01:45.020
Hardships and Difficulties
Tiberius Julius Caesar was born on November

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:47.680
16 in the year 42 BCE.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:52.070
This was two years after the assassination
of Julius Caesar, and young Tiberius’ childhood

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:55.440
would carry on under the shadow of the civil
war that followed it.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:00.460
His father’s name was Tiberius Claudius
Nero, and his mother was called Livia Drusilla.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:05.229
Tiberius senior had been an officer under
Julius Caesar but had sided with Mark Anthony.

00:02:05.229 --> 00:02:09.780
For this reason, the young family lived in
exile, constantly in fear of having their

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.670
hands, tongues or other body parts handed
back to them by Caesar Augustus.

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:18.430
The Historian Suetonius, renowned for his
prose and body odour, wrote this of Tiberius’

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:21.720
early years:
“His childhood and youth were beset with

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:26.430
hardships and difficulties because Nero and
Livia took him wherever they went in their

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:28.020
flight from Augustus.”

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:32.330
For example, when the family was hiding in
Naples, Octavian’s men broke into the city,

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:35.170
and they had to make a quiet escape through
the port.

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:39.680
But the infant Tiberius cried so loud that
he nearly betrayed the whole party.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.220
After Naples, the family moved to Sicily and
eventually to Sparta.

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:48.870
but Augustus still would not let go, and during
a night time escape Livia scorched her hair

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:50.920
while running througha forest fire.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:55.620
Luckily for the toddler, when he turned three,
Augustus proclaimed amnesty for the conspirators.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:59.920
The family returned to Rome in 39 BCE, upon
which Livia took a leaf from her husband’s

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:01.790
book and pulled a switcheroo.

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:06.420
She divorced Tiberius Nero and set her eyes
on a new husband that could grant a rosy future

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:09.750
for her and her son: Emperor Augustus.

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:12.819
Three months after their marriage, Livia delivered
another son: Drusus.

00:03:12.819 --> 00:03:19.720
If for a moment you thought that the father
was Octavian, that would make Livia a porcupine

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:21.651
and Drusus a premature baby porcupine.

00:03:21.651 --> 00:03:23.072
[Caption: The gestation period of a porcupine
is 112 days]

00:03:23.072 --> 00:03:26.690
Tiberius’ baby brother had in fact been
conceived with Tiberius senior.

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:31.540
This is important: Livia had no children with
Octavian, which meant that she had to work

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:34.629
extra hard to secure the advancement of her
sons.

00:03:34.629 --> 00:03:39.620
In point of fact, Octavian Augustus had been
preparing for his own two grandsons, Gaius

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:42.310
Caesar and Lucius Caesar to succeed him.

00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:47.439
Gaius and Lucius were the sons of Augustus’
daughter Julia and Augustus’ right hand

00:03:47.439 --> 00:03:49.579
man Marcus Agrippa.

00:03:49.579 --> 00:03:53.250
Even though the parents of the two boys were
alive and kicking, Augustus had decided to

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:57.530
adopt them to better groom them for succession,
a common practice at that time.

00:03:57.530 --> 00:04:01.159
Livia’s efforts to supplant the order of
succession seemed to pay dividends.

00:04:01.159 --> 00:04:05.760
In his early adolescence, Tiberius had the
privilege to organise some gladiatorial games

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:06.760
in the Forum.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:10.760
More importantly, he was allowed to ride on
Augustus’ chariot at his triumph following

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:12.209
the Battle of Actium.

00:04:12.209 --> 00:04:16.780
At age 18, Tiberius was appointed quaestor,
an entry level magistrate with the responsibility

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:21.130
of collecting taxes or even recruiting soldiers
in conquered territories.

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:24.480
His skill and energy soon led to a military
command.

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:32.660
A Tribunus and a Gentleman
In the year 22 BCE, a twenty-year old Tiberius

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:38.860
was dispatched to Iberia as a Tribunus Laticlavius,
an officer who served as second-in-command

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:41.160
to the Legatus, the head of a Legion.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:44.200
In this function, he served in the northern
region of Cantabria.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:48.560
The locals had rebelled against Rome, and
Tiberius restored peace by decimating them.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:53.760
This success in Spain granted him a more prestigious
assignment: Tiberius was now put at the head

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:58.760
of a Legion and sent to Armenia in 20 BCE
to install a king friendly to Rome.

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:03.820
The neighbouring Parthians, who would be modern-day
Iranians, were not amused and threatened war.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:08.510
Tiberius displayed some good negotiating skills,
agreeing to a compromise: a neutral king would

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:12.850
rule Armenia, making it a buffer state between
Rome and Parthia.

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:17.070
In exchange, the Parthians would return three
Legionary battle standards seized from Marcus

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:19.010
Crassus at the battle of Carrhae.

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:22.480
Tiberius returned to Rome with three Eagles
and was hailed as a hero.

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:23.480
I did something similar yesterday.

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:24.480
I returned home with three chickens and was
hailed by my cats.

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:28.410
In 19 BCE Tiberius married his first wife,
Vipsania, whom he loved dearly.

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:33.330
And I realise that by calling her ‘first’
wife I sort of spoiled what’s coming next.

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:37.660
According to Suetonius, Vipsania was the daughter
of Marcus Agrippa.

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:40.140
And who was Agrippas’ wife?

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:43.230
Julia, daughter of Augustus.

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:47.700
I have done some math, here:
Julia was born in 39 BCE and she married Agrippa

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:49.530
in 21 BCE.

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:50.530
Age 18.

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:51.530
So far, so good.

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:53.300
Agrippa died in the year 12 BCE.

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:57.710
In those nine years they were married, these
two would have a total of five children, with

00:05:57.710 --> 00:05:59.360
one of them being Vipsania.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:03.620
Based on this timeline, Vipsania should have
been 2 when she married Tiberius.

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:09.430
Suetonius does not offer clarity, so I am
going to assume, and hope, that Vipsania was

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:14.020
a daughter from a previous marriage, and that
the name just ran in the family.

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:17.440
Another name that ran in the family was Drusus,
Tiberius’ brother.

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:21.060
When the newlyweds had a son, they, too, named
their child Drusus.

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:25.900
Because why choose a new name and miss a chance
to complicate a historian’s work?

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:28.420
What historians do get right is the military
campaigns.

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:33.390
We know that Tiberius’ military adventures
continued in 15 BCE, when he joined little

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:37.540
brother Drusus in Gaul, or as Suetonius called
it

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:39.240
'the Long-Haired province.’

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:43.850
Here, feuds between the Gallic chieftains
had caused considerable unrest, but Tiberius

00:06:43.850 --> 00:06:46.950
brought his own brand of Iberian peace.

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:49.680
After that he fought in the Alps, Austria,
and Germany.

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:54.060
During these campaigns he ensured that the
local population bend the knee to Rome, securing

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:56.220
the northern borders of the Empire.

00:06:56.220 --> 00:07:00.470
During his campaign in Germany he captured
some 40,000 German prisoners, whom he forcibly

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:03.860
relocated in Gaul put an end to their constant
revolts.

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:08.470
Eight centuries later, Charlemagne would apply
the exact same tactic to quash the Saxons.

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:13.490
All the while, mother Livia had continued
her machinations to ensure the career progression

00:07:13.490 --> 00:07:14.600
of Tiberius.

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:19.520
She had set her eye on the highest prize:
succession to the throne of Emperor.

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:23.940
Tiberius went along with these schemes, though
we don’t know how much of it he participated

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:24.940
in voluntarily.

00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:30.710
In 12 BCE, Augustus agreed to consider him
as a successor, but only if Tiberius would

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:34.630
divorce his beloved wife and marry Julia,
recently widowed.

00:07:34.630 --> 00:07:36.350
Tiberius was not happy about this.

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:41.740
First, Vipsania was pregnant again, and he
knew of Julia’s adulterous past.

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:44.350
Even Augustus knew of her adulterous past.

00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:47.500
Heck, all of Rome knew of her adulterous past.

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:52.580
Surely, men weren’t saints either, but hey,
double standards are not a recent invention.

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:57.630
Julia, in fact, had even tried to seduce Tiberius
while she was still married to his father-in-law

00:07:57.630 --> 00:07:58.630
Agrippa.

00:07:58.630 --> 00:07:59.630
Wait!

00:07:59.630 --> 00:08:01.870
This makes of Julia … his step-mother-in-law.

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:03.300
Trying to seduce him?

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:06.210
We are in full soap opera mode now!

00:08:06.210 --> 00:08:09.960
The throne was the throne and eventually Tiberius
relented, marrying Julia.

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:13.960
Neither of them was particularly happy about
this union, but initially, they tried to make

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:14.960
it work.

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:17.040
Over time, Tiberius began to loathe her.

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:22.940
When their first and only child died in infancy,
he completely retreated from her.

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:29.060
Bumpy Rhodes
Tiberius did more than just retreat.

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:33.430
He went into a voluntary exile on the island
of Rhodes in 6 BCE.

00:08:33.430 --> 00:08:37.060
Julia went on to have an affair with Mark
Anthony’s son, and this was politically

00:08:37.060 --> 00:08:38.190
dangerous.

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:41.360
Eventually Augustus banished her from Rome
in 2 BCE.

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:46.579
The relationship with Julia may have been
the main reason for his exile, but even Suetonius

00:08:46.579 --> 00:08:47.920
is not sure.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:51.529
Another theory is related to Augustus’ grandchildren,
Gaius and Lucius.

00:08:51.529 --> 00:08:56.120
They had now come of age, and Tiberius did
not want to stand in the way of their lawful

00:08:56.120 --> 00:08:57.120
succession.

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:01.589
The implication here is that he was a reluctant
aspiring Emperor, to say the least.

00:09:01.589 --> 00:09:05.800
During his self-imposed time in Rhodes, Tiberius
visited the sick, worked as a magistrate,

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:06.949
and mingled with the locals.

00:09:06.949 --> 00:09:11.990
He also attended philosophy lectures and debates,
which could occasionally get loud and out

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:12.990
of hand.

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:15.600
During one of these debates, he was accused
by a student of supporting one philosopher

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:16.649
over the other.

00:09:16.649 --> 00:09:21.290
Instead of mounting a well-reasoned comeback,
the normally-unflappable Tiberius went home,

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:22.910
only to return with some guards.

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:26.120
He had the loudmouth seized and sent to the
gaol.

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:30.680
After the first few years of voluntary exile,
Tiberius started regretting his decision.

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:32.529
He longed to return home.

00:09:32.529 --> 00:09:34.470
But Augustus would not give his permission.

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:38.959
Slanderous rumours had started circulating:
apparently ‘The Exile,’ as he was now

00:09:38.959 --> 00:09:41.110
known, was plotting against the Emperor.

00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:45.839
Eventually it was Gaius who convinced his
grandfather to yield, and Tiberius was allowed

00:09:45.839 --> 00:09:53.170
back to Rome in 2 CE, on one condition: Tiberius
renounce all interest in politics and lead

00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:55.029
an apolitical life.

00:09:55.029 --> 00:09:56.550
This would prove to be difficult.

00:09:56.550 --> 00:10:01.060
He had barely set foot in Rome when Lucius,
younger of the Emperor’s heirs, died of

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:03.209
an illness while travelling to Iberia.

00:10:03.209 --> 00:10:07.639
A year and a half later, the older Prince,
Gaius, died from wounds received in Armenia.

00:10:07.639 --> 00:10:13.040
Augustus was now left with only one male descendant,
Agrippa Postumus, youngest son of Julia, and

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:14.040
still a boy.

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:18.079
Four months later, Augustus formally adopted
Tiberius, a clear sign that Tiberius would

00:10:18.079 --> 00:10:19.670
be his principal successor.

00:10:19.670 --> 00:10:24.620
But for the moment, the Emperor had other
plans in mind: Tiberius was needed in Germany.

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:28.970
In the ensuing campaigns, the valorous General
Tiberius quashed another rebellion, then moved

00:10:28.970 --> 00:10:32.480
to Bohemia, Pannonia and Dalmatia to deal
with further insurrections.

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:37.300
When he was about to celebrate his well-won
triumphs, news reached Rome of the terrible

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:43.449
catastrophe at Teutoburg Forest, 9CE, in which
Germanic tribes had annihilated the three

00:10:43.449 --> 00:10:45.360
legions of General Varo.

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:50.060
Tiberius returned to Germany for two more
expeditions, alongside his nephew Germanicus,

00:10:50.060 --> 00:10:54.839
but Augustus had wisely decided not to expand
further into those lands.

00:10:54.839 --> 00:11:02.720
A reluctant Emperor
In the year 14 CE, Tiberius was about to depart

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:05.820
for yet another campaign when he was summoned
by Augustus.

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:08.160
The terminally ill Emperor asked him not to
leave.

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:13.160
It was clear to Tiberius that succession was
around the corner: this was the moment that

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:15.389
his mother Livia had been waiting for.

00:11:15.389 --> 00:11:20.839
Augustus clearly valued Tiberius administrative
and military skills, yet he disliked his dour,

00:11:20.839 --> 00:11:21.839
sombre character.

00:11:21.839 --> 00:11:28.010
Tiberius had a quiet, inscrutable demeanour,
but could unleash retribution when least expected.

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:32.800
After their last meeting, Augustus gasped
‘"Poor Rome, doomed to be masticated by

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:34.879
those slow-moving jaws!'

00:11:34.879 --> 00:11:39.529
When Augustus died, apparently Tiberius and
Livia did not break the news immediately.

00:11:39.529 --> 00:11:43.910
This is disputed, but Livia may have ordered
the assassination of the young Agrippa, the

00:11:43.910 --> 00:11:46.610
remaining living grandson of Augustus.

00:11:46.610 --> 00:11:50.430
According to this version, after Livia got
rid of the competition, Tiberius summoned

00:11:50.430 --> 00:11:53.069
the Senate and announced the death of the
Emperor.

00:11:53.069 --> 00:11:54.759
This meeting was a strange affair.

00:11:54.759 --> 00:11:57.790
Tiberius started a speech, but could not go
on, claiming that

00:11:57.790 --> 00:12:03.309
‘grief had robbed him of his voice and that
he wished his life would also be taken’.

00:12:03.309 --> 00:12:07.759
The Senate then read Augustus’ will, in
which he made it clear that Tiberius was only

00:12:07.759 --> 00:12:10.950
his third choice of Emperor behind Gaius and
Lucius.

00:12:10.950 --> 00:12:15.620
When the Senate formally asked him if he wanted
to take the mantle, he visibly hesitated and

00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:16.990
remained silent.

00:12:16.990 --> 00:12:21.059
He did accept eventually, becoming the new
Emperor but leaving an impression that he

00:12:21.059 --> 00:12:23.040
was doing so only reluctantly.

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:24.319
This may be true.

00:12:24.319 --> 00:12:26.610
After all, it was Livia’s main goal, not
his.

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:30.759
He had proved to be more at ease with military
rather than civilian life and was prone to

00:12:30.759 --> 00:12:33.300
seek isolation for long periods.

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:37.730
According to other interpretations, Tiberius
was just trying to emulate Augustus.

00:12:37.730 --> 00:12:42.149
This is exactly what Augustus would have done
-- feign humility before accepting the highest

00:12:42.149 --> 00:12:43.499
possible honours.

00:12:43.499 --> 00:12:48.390
As an Emperor, Tiberius’ first act was to
rescind the allowance for hjs estranged wife

00:12:48.390 --> 00:12:49.390
Julia.

00:12:49.390 --> 00:12:53.079
The results were brutal, and poor Julia died
of malnutrition as a result.

00:12:53.079 --> 00:12:57.890
This was a typical example of Tiberius’
ability to exact cold and calculated retribution

00:12:57.890 --> 00:13:00.149
on his enemies when he needed to.

00:13:00.149 --> 00:13:04.579
His first years in power, however, were stable,
prosperous and peaceful to Roman citizens.

00:13:04.579 --> 00:13:09.850
This may have been a consequence of Tiberius
getting rid of his scheming mother Livia.

00:13:09.850 --> 00:13:14.399
According to historian Cassius Dio, Livia
did not miss a chance to declare that it was

00:13:14.399 --> 00:13:19.140
she who had made Tiberius emperor; not only
did she wish to rule in equal terms with him,

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:22.320
but she wanted her decisions to take precedence
over his.

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:26.540
Tiberius, who had once been a pawn in her
hand, warned Livia not to interfere in the

00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:29.779
running of the state, removing her from public
affairs.

00:13:29.779 --> 00:13:32.480
He refrained from having any future contact
with her.

00:13:32.480 --> 00:13:36.540
Tiberius initiated a series of public works,
while always keeping an eye on expenses.

00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:41.009
He tried to maintain a good relationship with
the Senate, respecting their authority.

00:13:41.009 --> 00:13:44.899
But his cold and impenetrable personality
did not gain him any friends, and he became

00:13:44.899 --> 00:13:46.230
increasingly unpopular.

00:13:46.230 --> 00:13:50.709
His polar opposite was his nephew, Germanicus,
by now also his adopted son.

00:13:50.709 --> 00:13:55.300
Germanicus was popular, well-liked and apparently
would have been the choice for Emperor in

00:13:55.300 --> 00:13:56.759
many a General’s mind.

00:13:56.759 --> 00:13:59.309
Tiberius had reason to fear a coup from Germanicus.

00:13:59.309 --> 00:14:03.319
The young officer, however, always voiced
his support of Tiberius.

00:14:03.319 --> 00:14:09.749
Bad Company
The drama escalated in 19 CE.

00:14:09.749 --> 00:14:13.170
On the 10th of October, Germanicus died in
Antioch after a brief illness.

00:14:13.170 --> 00:14:15.569
His body showed potential signs of poisoning.

00:14:15.569 --> 00:14:20.149
His wife was Agrippina the Elder, who was
also a granddaughter of Augustus.

00:14:20.149 --> 00:14:24.470
Agrippina immediately accused Tiberius of
having ordered the assassination of Germanicus.

00:14:24.470 --> 00:14:28.029
According to her, Tiberius had exploited the
enmity between Germanicus and one Gnaeus Piso,

00:14:28.029 --> 00:14:32.519
a former governor of Syria who had been ousted
by the young General.

00:14:32.519 --> 00:14:37.470
Piso was tried in Rome, and despite his appeals
to Tiberius, he was forced to commit suicide.

00:14:37.470 --> 00:14:41.610
Forced suicide was a form of capital punishment
sometimes used in Imperial Rome, another notable

00:14:41.610 --> 00:14:44.600
victim being philosopher Seneca during Nero’s
reign.

00:14:44.600 --> 00:14:47.520
Agrippina was not satisfied with Piso’s
execution.

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:52.360
She made it clear to the Emperor that with
Germanicus dead, her three sons, Nero Caesar,

00:14:52.360 --> 00:14:56.220
Drusus Caesar, and Gaius Julius Caesar, also
had a claim to the throne.

00:14:56.220 --> 00:14:58.769
True to character, Tiberius did not react
initially.

00:14:58.769 --> 00:15:00.839
He even took a liking to the boys.

00:15:00.839 --> 00:15:04.790
But in Suetonius’ version of the events,
he took action at a later stage.

00:15:04.790 --> 00:15:08.360
He first brought about all sorts of false
charges against the two eldest, Nero and Drusus.

00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:11.839
When they dared complain, he had them marked
as public enemies by the Senate.

00:15:11.839 --> 00:15:16.339
This led to their incarceration and eventual
death: Nero was forced to commit suicide and

00:15:16.339 --> 00:15:18.269
Drusus starved to death.

00:15:18.269 --> 00:15:22.240
The boy was so hungry that he had started
eating his mattress.

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:26.860
Of Germanicus’ sons, only Gaius would survive,
and he was actually welcomed into Tiberius’

00:15:26.860 --> 00:15:27.860
household.

00:15:27.860 --> 00:15:32.160
Gaius used to wear a miniature uniform, while
on campaign with his father, hence his surname:

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:33.779
‘little boots,’ or Caligula.

00:15:33.779 --> 00:15:38.149
We have a video about him, by the way, so
go and watch it later!

00:15:38.149 --> 00:15:39.649
Just not quite yet.

00:15:39.649 --> 00:15:41.790
There are more conspiracies coming.

00:15:41.790 --> 00:15:46.449
The death of Germanicus brought a change to
Tiberius’ personality; according to Cassius

00:15:46.449 --> 00:15:50.180
Dio, he became increasingly cruel
“… toward those who were suspected of

00:15:50.180 --> 00:15:54.750
plotting against him he was inexorable…slaves
were tortured to make them testify against

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:56.139
their own masters…”

00:15:56.139 --> 00:16:00.100
Suetonius agreed with this change of demeanour:
“Tiberius did so many other wicked deeds

00:16:00.100 --> 00:16:05.170
under the pretext of reforming public morals,
but in reality (it was) to gratify his lust

00:16:05.170 --> 00:16:06.990
for seeing people suffer.”

00:16:06.990 --> 00:16:11.550
During this period, Tiberius became best buddies
with an officer of the legion, Lucius Aelius

00:16:11.550 --> 00:16:12.550
Sejanus.

00:16:12.550 --> 00:16:16.730
Eventually, he appointed him Prefect, or commander,
of the Praetorian Guard.

00:16:16.730 --> 00:16:20.420
This was the elite corps tasked with protecting
the Emperor and his family.

00:16:20.420 --> 00:16:24.100
Sejanus grew in standing, becoming the chief
adviser and confidante of Tiberius.

00:16:24.100 --> 00:16:27.850
Tiberius gradually withdrew from the day to
day running of the Empire, delegating many

00:16:27.850 --> 00:16:29.449
decisions to Sejanus.

00:16:29.449 --> 00:16:34.540
Sejanus started seeing himself as the next
Emperor and began to view Drusus, son of Tiberius

00:16:34.540 --> 00:16:36.709
and Vipsania, as a natural rival.

00:16:36.709 --> 00:16:41.059
In order to strike at him, Sejanus hit Drusus
where it hurt the most: he began an affair

00:16:41.059 --> 00:16:42.369
with his wife, Livillia.

00:16:42.369 --> 00:16:45.389
Drusus died in 23 CE by poisoning.

00:16:45.389 --> 00:16:48.519
It is very likely that he was killed by Sejanus
and Livillia.

00:16:48.519 --> 00:16:52.949
This was not immediately evident, though,
and the grieving Tiberius launched a harsh

00:16:52.949 --> 00:16:55.040
investigation to find the culprits.

00:16:55.040 --> 00:16:59.730
He became so enraged and ruthless that according
to Suetonius he once mistook a friendly visitor

00:16:59.730 --> 00:17:01.540
from Rhodes as a key witness.

00:17:01.540 --> 00:17:04.290
He had him seized, interrogated, even tortured.

00:17:04.290 --> 00:17:06.680
When it was clear that the poor guy had just
popped in to say ‘Ave’

00:17:06.680 --> 00:17:10.070
{Ahh-Vay}, Tiberius had him executed to silence
the scandal.

00:17:10.070 --> 00:17:15.380
By 25 CE Sejanus had grown the strength of
the Praetorian Guard to 12000 men and had

00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:17.860
golden statues of him erected around Rome.

00:17:17.860 --> 00:17:22.410
Next, he embarked on a series of treason trials
to weed out any possible opposition.

00:17:22.410 --> 00:17:25.120
By this point, many Romans were living in
fear.

00:17:25.120 --> 00:17:29.320
At Livillia’s insistence, Sejanus divorced
his wife and left his children; the couple

00:17:29.320 --> 00:17:34.950
then appealed to Tiberius for permission to
marry, but Tiberius denied the request.

00:17:34.950 --> 00:17:42.090
A Second Exile
Tiberius grew more and more distant from the

00:17:42.090 --> 00:17:43.340
affairs of the Empire.

00:17:43.340 --> 00:17:47.431
As he had done years before, he decided to
withdraw into a second virtual exile, this

00:17:47.431 --> 00:17:50.860
time moving to the isle of Capri in 26 CE.

00:17:50.860 --> 00:17:53.410
This did not quell his thirst for blood, according
to Suetonius.

00:17:53.410 --> 00:17:59.120
I should specify that Suetonius himself used
as sources the memoirs of Agrippina the Younger.

00:17:59.120 --> 00:18:00.120
Who was she?

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:05.750
She was the daughter of Germanicus, so her
writings may have been kind of anti-Tiberius

00:18:05.750 --> 00:18:09.560
... During the years in Capri, Tiberius had
countless alleged conspirators thrown to their

00:18:09.560 --> 00:18:11.960
death from the rocks high above the sea.

00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:15.700
In one famous incident, a fisherman with a
huge mullet visited Tiberius.

00:18:15.700 --> 00:18:17.760
Not the hairstyle, the fish.

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:19.580
He was bringing the fish as a present.

00:18:19.580 --> 00:18:23.570
Tiberius was so scared and paranoid that he
ordered his guards to rub the fisherman's

00:18:23.570 --> 00:18:24.980
face with the mullet.

00:18:24.980 --> 00:18:27.560
The scales skinned it raw, and the poor guy
shouted:

00:18:27.560 --> 00:18:32.940
'Thank Heaven, I did not bring Caesar that
huge crab I also caught!'

00:18:32.940 --> 00:18:36.760
Tiberius sent for the crab and had it used
in the same way.

00:18:36.760 --> 00:18:41.870
In the year 29, news reached Tiberius that
his mother Livia had died, aged 86.

00:18:41.870 --> 00:18:45.120
She had been suffering from a long illness,
but Tiberius had never visited her.

00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:49.620
Even in death, he basically ignored her, and
made no arrangements at all in her honour,

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:53.560
except for the bare minimum: a public funeral
… which he did not even attend!

00:18:53.560 --> 00:18:57.990
This is according to historian Tacitus – another
guy we should take cum grano salis

00:18:57.990 --> 00:19:01.350
[Caption: with a pinch of salt]
As he was staunchly pro-Senate and wary of

00:19:01.350 --> 00:19:02.350
Imperial power.

00:19:02.350 --> 00:19:05.050
He wrote that Tiberius excused himself from
the funeral

00:19:05.050 --> 00:19:08.120
“… on the ground of the pressure of business.”

00:19:08.120 --> 00:19:11.010
Two years later, Caligula moved to Capri to
live with Tiberius.

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:15.010
If the reports from our ancient historians
are true, then we could say that the future

00:19:15.010 --> 00:19:20.810
mad ruler Caligula may have learned a trick
or two from the Tiberius Playbook of Cruelty.

00:19:20.810 --> 00:19:23.640
But in some cases, his ruthlessness may have
had a point.

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:29.000
In the same year, 31 CE, Sejanus and Livillia
announced their betrothal – they would go

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:31.190
ahead, with or without Imperial permission.

00:19:31.190 --> 00:19:35.750
Livillia’s mother, Antonia Minor, wrote
to Tiberius and informed him that the lovebirds

00:19:35.750 --> 00:19:38.370
had a plan to murder him and young Caligula.

00:19:38.370 --> 00:19:42.440
Tiberius rushed back to Rome and appeared
before the Senate, demanding that Sejanus

00:19:42.440 --> 00:19:44.140
be subject to trial.

00:19:44.140 --> 00:19:48.350
Sejanus was lured to the Senate and forced
to answer to the accusations of conspiracy

00:19:48.350 --> 00:19:49.490
to commit murder.

00:19:49.490 --> 00:19:53.580
Under the Emperor’s influence, it was a
swift trial: the Praetorian was found guilty

00:19:53.580 --> 00:19:54.960
and condemned to death.

00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:56.610
Not just any death.

00:19:56.610 --> 00:20:01.780
Sejanus was strangled and thrown onto an assembled
mob, who tore his limbs apart and fed his

00:20:01.780 --> 00:20:02.870
remains to the dogs.

00:20:02.870 --> 00:20:05.810
His sons and followers were also executed.

00:20:05.810 --> 00:20:09.640
Livillia was also put to death, with what
I now believe to be Tiberius’ favourite

00:20:09.640 --> 00:20:12.010
modus operandi: starvation.

00:20:12.010 --> 00:20:18.290
Her own mother Antonia made sure she did not
eat anything.

00:20:18.290 --> 00:20:23.980
Legacy
The reclusion of Tiberius in Capri became

00:20:23.980 --> 00:20:26.800
a full-time status in his later years.

00:20:26.800 --> 00:20:31.550
Now in his 70s and increasingly ill, he attempted
to return to Rome only twice after the trial

00:20:31.550 --> 00:20:32.550
of Sejanus.

00:20:32.550 --> 00:20:36.130
In the first occasion, he was travelling by
trireme up the Tiber, but upon seeing the

00:20:36.130 --> 00:20:38.930
City walls from a distance, he ordered the
captain to return to Capri.

00:20:38.930 --> 00:20:42.880
In the second occasion, he was travelling
by land when he noticed that his pet snake

00:20:42.880 --> 00:20:44.770
had been eaten alive by ants.

00:20:44.770 --> 00:20:48.520
Interpreting this as an omen of a mob rebellion,
he retreated once again.

00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:52.810
Tiberius died on March 16, in the year 37
CE, at the age of 77.

00:20:52.810 --> 00:20:57.300
It may have been illness; it may have been
poison administered by a new Praetorian prefect,

00:20:57.300 --> 00:20:59.060
with help from Caligula.

00:20:59.060 --> 00:21:02.210
Upon hearing of his death, the Romans, according
to Suetonius, yelled

00:21:02.210 --> 00:21:07.200
“To the Tiber with Tiberius”
Cassius Dio, in a more balanced account, simply

00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:09.770
wrote:
“Thus Tiberius, who possessed a great many

00:21:09.770 --> 00:21:15.080
virtues, and a great many vices, and followed
each set in turn as if the other did not exist,

00:21:15.080 --> 00:21:18.930
passed away in this fashion on the twenty-sixth
day of March.”

00:21:18.930 --> 00:21:23.370
Apart from getting the date wrong, Cassius
summarises the lights and shadows of the reign

00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:25.030
and personality of Tiberius.

00:21:25.030 --> 00:21:28.510
As we have seen today, Tiberius had a cold
and calculating intellect.

00:21:28.510 --> 00:21:32.980
He could be a good leader of men in the field,
but was often uneasy when it came to networking

00:21:32.980 --> 00:21:34.540
with the political class.

00:21:34.540 --> 00:21:39.790
He alternated periods of intense, paranoid
and psychotic activity with total withdrawal

00:21:39.790 --> 00:21:40.870
from society.

00:21:40.870 --> 00:21:46.150
He was a puzzle within a mystery to his contemporaries,
but can we do something more to better understand

00:21:46.150 --> 00:21:47.150
him?

00:21:47.150 --> 00:21:51.420
Two of his successors, Caligula and Claudius,
have been analysed and diagnosed as potentially

00:21:51.420 --> 00:21:53.180
having a bipolar disorder.

00:21:53.180 --> 00:21:58.920
Tiberius, Caligula and Claudius were all related,
having common ancestors in Livia and Tiberius

00:21:58.920 --> 00:21:59.920
senior.

00:21:59.920 --> 00:22:04.860
Considering that this disorder can be hereditary,
could it be possible that also Tiberius had

00:22:04.860 --> 00:22:05.860
the same affliction?

00:22:05.860 --> 00:22:09.570
I would not offer any conclusion -- I’ll
leave it to you in the comments.

00:22:09.570 --> 00:22:12.560
I do hope you like today’s video.

00:22:12.560 --> 00:22:27.940
Please like, share and subscribe and as usual
… thank you for watching!

